  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nLarge upper level closed low pressure system is now moving across\nCalifornia and western Nevada, and will continue to slowly make \nits way across the state tonight and into Friday. The closed low \ncurrently over Santa Barbara, will weaken as it moves into Nevada \neventually opening up into a open trough by Friday morning. The \nresultant longwave trough will not be long for the world however \nas the energy splits the trough into two pieces by Saturday \nmorning. One piece will form a new upper low over southern \nCalifornia, while the northern section lifts north into Montana. \nSo for the rest of tonight and into Friday there will be several \nrounds of scattered showers and a thunderstorms with a marginal \nrisk for a few severe thunderstorms for eastern Nevada, as the \ndynamic upper level lift and instability will reside over NV. As \nthe trough moves more overhead tonight and into Friday morning \ncooler air aloft will try and filter in, this will take snow \nlevels, which have been near 9000 feet for majority of time, and \ndrop to about 7000 feet. Timing may allow for snowfall which had \nbeen restricted to the mountain summits, to reach some of the \nhigher elevation passes. With Austin summit, Mountain City, \nRobinson, and Pinto as totals look to range from a trace to 0.5\u201d. \nHowever the range peaks look to see the best snow chances with up \nto a 18\u201d possible above 9000 feet across central and northern NV. \nRainfall amounts look to be good with this system as most location\nlook to pick up from 0.15\u201d to 0.75\u201d in the valleys and passes, \nwith up to 2.00\u201d of liquid for the mountains through Friday \nafternoon. Models have been hinting at an band of enhanced \nprecipitation may try and set up over western Humboldt county with\nsome accumulations there reaching up to 1.50\u201d through Friday. As \nof now this band look to stay just to the west over far eastern \nCalifornia, but will keep an eye out to see if there will be any \nflood potential shifts east tonight. The upper trough and new low \nlook to push east Saturday which will allow for high pressure to \nquickly build in on the heels of this weeks system. Models show \nthat this ridge will be firmly in control by Sunday, which will \nusher in dry quiet weather with cooler temperatures in the upper \n50s to upper 60s for highs and cool nights in the upper 20s to \nupper 30s through next Thursday. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for the potential for several rounds of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms starting this afternoon, and \npersisting on and off through Friday morning.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in the probability of a few severe \nstorms developing this afternoon across Elko and White Pine \nCounties, as the SPC has a marginal risk highlighted for the area,\nwith strong outflow winds being the primary hazard with storms. \n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Saturday \nthrough Wednesday of next week which will bring quiet, dry \nconditions for the region.",
